  "title": "Convert Binary Number in a Linked List to Integer",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Linked list",
  "patterns": [
    "linked list",
    "bit manipulation"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "what's the relationship between a binary digit's position and its contribution to the decimal value?",
    "if you already have the decimal value of everything before the current bit, how does seeing one more bit update that value?",
    "each new bit doubles the running value (shifts everything one place left) and then adds the new bit",
    "start result = 0, and for each value in nums do result = result * 2 + value"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "processing the bits in the wrong order (least significant first instead of most significant first)",
    "using string concatenation and parseInt without handling potential overflow-style thinking, or overcomplicating with pow()",
    "off-by-one in exponent calculation when computing powers of two directly instead of using the running-total trick"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]` is\nthe value of the i-th node in order. Every node's value is either 0 or\n1, and read from head to tail the nodes spell out a binary number, most\nsignificant bit first.\n\n**Return the decimal value of that binary number.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 30",
      "every value in nums is 0 or 1",
      "the list represents a number without leading-zero ambiguity issues (leading zeros are simply worth 0)"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list of 0",
      "a single-node list of 1",
      "leading zeros, e.g. [0, 1, 1]"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 0, 1]",
        "output": "5",
        "expectedOutput": "5",
        "explanation": "binary 101 is decimal 5"
      },
      {
